---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `let i = 1;

while (i <= 5) {
  console.log("Pass", i);
  i = i + 1;
}`;
const Figure2 = `for (let i = 1; i <= 5; i = i + 1) {
  console.log("Pass", i);
}`;
const Figure3 = `Pass 1
Pass 2
Pass 3
Pass 4
Pass 5`;
const Figure4 = `for (let i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
  console.log(i);
}`;
const Figure5 = `for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  console.log(i);
}`;
const Figure6 = `0
1
2
3
4`;
const NotationJs = `for (let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
  console.log(i);
}`;
const NotationPseudo = `i ← 1
REPEAT 5 TIMES
{
  DISPLAY(i)
  i ← i + 1
}`;
const Task1 = `for (let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
  console.log("Checking system", i);
}

console.log("All systems checked.");
`;
const Task2 = `for (let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
  console.log("Checking system", i);
}

console.log("All systems checked.");
console.log("");

let total = 0;

for (let i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
  total = total + i;
}

console.log("Sum of 1 to 10:", total);

for (let i = 10; i >= 1; i--) {
  console.log("T-minus", i);
}
`;
const Task3 = `for (let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
  console.log("Checking system", i);
}

console.log("All systems checked.");
console.log("");

let total = 0;

for (let i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
  total = total + i;
}

console.log("Sum of 1 to 10:", total);

for (let i = 10; i >= 1; i--) {
  console.log("T-minus", i);
}

console.log("");

// Ask the user how many crates to load, and cast it
// Loop that many times, showing the crate number each pass
// Keep a running total of mass, adding 40 per crate
// After the loop, show the total mass
`;
const ExampleOutput = `Checking system 1
Checking system 2
Checking system 3
Checking system 4
Checking system 5
All systems checked.

Sum of 1 to 10: 55
T-minus 10
T-minus 9
T-minus 8
T-minus 7
T-minus 6
T-minus 5
T-minus 4
T-minus 3
T-minus 2
T-minus 1

How many crates? 3
Crate 1 loaded
Crate 2 loaded
Crate 3 loaded
Total mass: 120
`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 1.19: Counting With for</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 1.19</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Counting With for</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Three Parts, One Line</p>
              <p>Counting Up and Down</p>
              <p>Starting at Zero</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>A Very Common Shape</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure1}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                Set up, test, change. You wrote a lot of these in Activity 1.17,
                and the three parts were scattered across three places.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>The Same Loop, Gathered Up</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure2}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
                class="mb-3"
              />
              <Code
                code={Figure3}
                frame="terminal"
                lang="txt"
                title="Demo"
                showLineNumbers={false}
                class="fragment"
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                Identical behavior. All three parts now sit on one line, in
                order, separated by semicolons.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Reading the Header</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt><CodeSnippet code="let i = 1" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /></dt>
                  <dd>runs once, before anything else</dd>
                  <dt><CodeSnippet code="i <= 5" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /></dt>
                  <dd>checked before every pass; false ends the loop</dd>
                  <dt><CodeSnippet code="i = i + 1" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /></dt>
                  <dd>runs after every pass</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
              <p class="fragment">
                Nothing new is happening. It is a <CodeSnippet code="while" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> loop with
                its parts tidied into one place, which makes forgetting one much
                harder.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>A Shorthand You Will See Everywhere</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure4}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                <CodeSnippet code="i++" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> means exactly{" "}
                <CodeSnippet code="i = i + 1" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />. <CodeSnippet code="i--" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> subtracts one.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Use whichever you find clearer. You need to <i>read</i> both.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Why Programmers Start at Zero</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure5}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
                class="mb-3"
              />
              <Code
                code={Figure6}
                frame="terminal"
                lang="txt"
                title="Demo"
                showLineNumbers={false}
                class="fragment"
              />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Why Programmers Start at Zero</h2>
              <p>
                <CodeSnippet code="0" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> to <CodeSnippet code="< 5" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> gives five passes: 0, 1, 2, 3, 4.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                It looks stranger than starting at 1, but it lines up with how
                lists are numbered in JavaScript &mdash; which is Activity 1.23's
                problem, and a big one.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Get used to reading <CodeSnippet code="i = 0; i < n" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> as "n times."
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Which Loop Should I Use?</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <ul>
                  <li class="fragment">
                    <b>for</b> &mdash; when you know how many passes before you
                    start
                  </li>
                  <li class="fragment">
                    <b>while</b> &mdash; when you are waiting for something to
                    become true
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <p class="fragment">
                Counting crates is a <CodeSnippet code="for" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />. Asking until the user
                types something valid is a <CodeSnippet code="while" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Writing a counted loop with for</li>
                <li>Reading the three parts of a for header</li>
                <li>Counting up and counting down</li>
                <li>Choosing between for and while</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Counted loop</dt>
                  <dd>A loop that runs a known number of times.</dd>
                  <dt>Loop counter</dt>
                  <dd>
                    The variable that tracks which pass the loop is on, usually
                    called <CodeSnippet code="i" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />.
                  </dd>
                  <dt>Increment</dt>
                  <dd>Adding one to a variable.</dd>
                  <dt>Decrement</dt>
                  <dd>Subtracting one from a variable.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Create a new <b>JavaScript</b> program named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">1-19-for</b>.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Five Passes</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Write the code below and run it.</li>
            <li>
              Change <CodeSnippet code="i <= 5" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> to{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="i < 5" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> and run again. Count the lines. One character,
              one fewer pass.
            </li>
            <li>Change it back before continuing.</li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task1}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Counting With for"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Accumulate, and Count Down</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Add a loop that sums 1 to 10, then one that counts backwards from
              10.
            </li>
            <li>
              The second one uses <CodeSnippet code="i--" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> and a{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code=">=" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> test. Going down means both the test and the
              change flip.
            </li>
            <li>
              Check the sum by hand &mdash; 1 through 10 adds up to 55.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">Note</b>: the green lines with a + sign
              are new code. Don't type the + sign.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task2}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Counting With for"
            ins={{ range: "6-18" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Plan, Then Loop Over Input</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              The comments describe a crate loader driven by a number the user
              types.
            </li>
            <li>
              The loop count comes from a variable, not a literal. That is the
              point &mdash; the program does not know how many passes until it
              runs.
            </li>
            <li>Cast the input, or your loop condition will misbehave.</li>
            <li>
              The running total must be declared <b>before</b> the loop. Declare
              it inside and it resets every pass.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task3}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Counting With for"
            ins={{ range: "20-25" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Challenge (Optional): Only the Even Ones</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Add a loop that displays only the even numbers from 1 to 20. There
              are two good ways to do it.
            </li>
            <li>
              One uses a conditional inside the loop and the remainder operator
              from Activity 1.6.
            </li>
            <li>
              The other changes the loop header so it never visits an odd number
              at all.
            </li>
            <li>
              Write both, then add a comment saying which you prefer and why.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Exam Notation -->
        <section id="exam-notation">
          <h2>Same Idea, Exam Notation</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">
              The reference sheet has no <code>for</code> loop at all.
            </b>{" "}
            It offers <code>REPEAT n TIMES</code>, <code>REPEAT UNTIL</code>, and
            one more you will meet at Activity 1.25. That is the complete set.
          </p>

          <div class="row g-3 my-4">
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={NotationJs}
                frame="code"
                lang="javascript"
                title="JavaScript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={NotationPseudo}
                frame="code"
                lang="txt"
                title="Exam Notation"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
          </div>

          <h4>Look at What Had to Be Added</h4>

          <p>
            <b>
              <code>REPEAT n TIMES</code> gives you no counter variable.
            </b>{" "}
            It repeats a block a set number of times and tells you nothing about
            which pass you are on.
          </p>

          <p>
            So when a program needs to know the pass number, the counter has to
            be built by hand &mdash; declared before the loop, and incremented
            as the last line inside it. Three lines of housekeeping that
            JavaScript packs into one header.
          </p>

          <h4 class="mt-4">Why This Matters on Exam Day</h4>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <b>This exact pattern appears constantly.</b> An initialization, a{" "}
              <code>REPEAT</code>, and an <code>i ← i + 1</code> at the bottom of
              the block is one of the most common shapes on the whole exam.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>The increment's position matters.</b> Put it before the work
              instead of after and every pass uses the wrong number.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Do not expect an <code>i</code> to be there.</b> If a question
              uses <code>REPEAT n TIMES</code> with no counter, the block genuinely
              cannot tell which pass it is on.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Verify your program works correctly.</p>

          <Code
            code={ExampleOutput}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            title="Example Output"
            mark={{
              label: "Number of passes decided at run time",
              range: "21-23",
            }}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Run the crate loader with 0 as the answer. It should load nothing and
            report a total of 0, without crashing. A loop that runs zero times is
            perfectly valid.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              A <code>for</code> loop and a <code>while</code> loop can do the
              same job. When is each one the clearer choice?
            </li>
            <li>
              <code>i &lt;= 5</code> and <code>i &lt; 5</code> differ by one
              character and one pass. Why is that such an easy mistake to make?
            </li>
            <li>
              Exam notation has no <code>for</code> loop, so a counter must be
              maintained by hand. What could go wrong if the{" "}
              <code>i ← i + 1</code> were placed at the <i>top</i> of the block
              instead of the bottom?
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
